
{"id":"274d5d61-1765-42de-88c7-22c173912b0d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784832697,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get('payload')\\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('Missing or invalid payload')\\n    nodes = payload.get('nodes')\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Missing or invalid nodes')\\n    required_symbols = payload.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Missing or invalid required_symbols')\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(node, dict):\\n            raise ValueError('Node not a dict')\\n        for key in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility'):\\n            if key not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Node missing field {key}')\\n        if not isinstance(node['id'], str):\\n            raise ValueError('id not string')\\n        if node['parent'] is not None and not isinstance(node['parent'], str):\\n            raise ValueError('parent not string or None')\\n        if not isinstance(node['symbol'], str):\\n            raise ValueError('symbol not string')\\n        if not isinstance(node['depth'], int):\\n            raise ValueError('depth not int')\\n        if not isinstance(node['removable'], bool):\\n            raise ValueError('removable not bool')\\n        if not isinstance(node['utility'], (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError('utility not number')\\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        p = node['parent']\\n        if p is None:\\n            if root_id is not None:\\n                raise ValueError('Multiple roots')\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n        else:\\n            if p not in node_map:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Parent {p} not found')\\n            children[p].append(node['id'])\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root')\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed_set:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if not all(cid in removed_set for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        retained = set(node_map.keys()) - removed_set - {nid}\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(node_map[rid]['symbol'] == sym for rid in retained):\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        removed_set.add(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set])\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is Python source code defining `solve(case)`. The code must implement a grammar-guided node reduction algorithm as described. The input `case` has a `payload` with `nodes` list and `required_symbols`. The output should be a JSON-serializable object with presumably \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". Need to parse requirements.\n\nFrom task description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" But we are not given actual grammar or interestingness oracle; rather, the \"registered properties\" like grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, etc are hard contracts. The code must simulate these? Actually we are to implement the algorithm that picks which nodes to remove based on depth, removable flag, required symbols, etc. The problem seems to be: given a tree (nodes with parent id, depth, symbol, removable flag, utility), and required_symbols list, we need to decide an order of removal of removable nodes such that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., at least one node with each required symbol remains). Also, cannot remove root or a node that has retained children? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Means: if a node is root (parent null) then can't remove. If a node's symbol is in required_symbols, then can't remove. If a node has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), then can't remove it because its children would become orphaned? But the tree is hierarchical; removing a node should also remove its descendants? Typically in parse tree reduction, removing a node removes its subtree. But the description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That suggests that we are allowed to remove a node only if all its children are also removed (or it has no children). So removal is bottom-up: you can only remove leaves (or nodes whose entire subtree is removed). Also \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes by descending depth, i.e., deepest first. At equal depth, use node ID as tie break (stable).\n\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That means before actually removing a node, we must check that after removal (and its whole subtree) all required symbols still appear in the tree (i.e., there is at least one node with that symbol not removed). So we need to simulate removal.\n\nReturn sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order. So output should have \"retained_ids\" (list of node IDs that remain after reduction) sorted in some order? Probably sorted by something? Not specified, but \"sorted retained IDs\" likely alphabetical/numerical? Could be sorted lexicographically as strings. We'll sort by node id.\n\nAlso \"actual removal order\" - list of node IDs in the order they were removed.\n\nNow we need to implement `solve(case)` that reads payload, builds tree structure, identifies nodes, processes them in order: all nodes sorted by depth descending (deeper first), then by node id ascending (stable tie break). For each node that is removable (removable=True) and not root (parent is not None? Actually root has parent null but also removable false, so skip), and not required symbol (symbol not in required_symbols), we tentatively consider removal. We must check if all required symbols are still present after removing this node and its entire subtree. If yes, then we remove it (mark as removed, add to removal order). Also we must ensure we are not removing a node that has children that are retained? But if we process bottom-up, children are deeper, so they would be processed before parent. If a child is removable and we removed it, then when processing parent, the child is already removed. However, if a child is not removable (e.g., because its symbol is required or it has its own children that are retained), then the parent cannot be removed because it would lose that child? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we need to check: for a given node, if any of its direct children (by parent pointer) are still retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove this node. So before removal, we need to check that all its children have been removed. This is automatically satisfied if we process bottom-up because deeper nodes are processed first; if a child is removable we would have removed it already. But if a child is not removable (e.g., required symbol), then parent cannot be removed because that child will never be removed. So we need to explicitly check: for node, if any child exists (in children list) that is not removed, then skip removal. So we need to maintain a set of removed IDs.\n\nAlso root cannot be removed. Also nodes with symbol in required_symbols cannot be removed.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n1. Parse case payload. Ensure nodes list is present and each node has required fields (id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility). Use ValueError if invalid.\n\n2. Build adjacency: for each node, store list of children by parent.\n\n3. Determine root: node with parent None.\n\n4. Initialize removed_set = set().\n\n5. Sort nodes: key = (-depth, id). (descending depth then ascending id)\n\n6. For each node in that order:\n   - If node.id in removed_set: skip (already removed as descendant of earlier removal? But we might have removed node as part of another removal? Actually we only remove nodes individually, not cascading? But removing a node also implicitly removes its entire subtree. So if we remove a node, we should mark all its descendants as removed as well. But the algorithm says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies removal of node and its subtree. So when we decide to remove a node, we need to also remove all descendants. However, the description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That suggests that we should only remove nodes that have no retained children. So we will only remove leaves or subtrees that are fully removed. To handle this properly, we should simulate: when we remove a node, we add it and all its descendants to removed_set. But we need to update children status. Since we process bottom-up, any descendant will be processed before its ancestor. So if a descendant is removable and we removed it, its id is in removed_set. Then when we consider ancestor, we see that all its children are in removed_set (if they were removed) or some are not removable but still present. But if a child is not removable (e.g., required symbol), it will never be in removed_set, so ancestor cannot be removed. So the check \"all children are removed\" can be done by verifying that for each child of node, child.id in removed_set or child is not removable? Actually we need that all children are either removed OR the child is not removable but we want to retain it? Wait: \"Never remove a node with retained children.\" That means if a node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove the node. So if a child is not removable (e.g., required), it will be retained, so parent cannot be removed. So condition: all children of node must be either already removed OR have been marked as removed? But we only mark removed when we actually remove. So to check, we need to see if for each child, its id is in removed_set (meaning it was removed as part of some earlier decision) OR if it is not in removed_set but it is not removable? That would mean it's retained but not removable. That would block parent removal. So simply: node can be removed only if all its children's id are in removed_set (i.e., they have been removed) or the node has no children. Because if a child is not removed, it's retained, so parent cannot be removed. So we check: all(child.id in removed_set for child in children_of_node). This ensures no retained children.\n\n   - Additionally, check that node.id not root and node.symbol not in required_symbols and node.removable is True.\n\n   - Then tentatively check if removing this node and its subtree would still leave all required symbols present. How to compute? We need to know which nodes are currently retained (not removed). After tentative removal, the retained set is all nodes except those in the subtree of node (including node). So we need to count presence of each required symbol among retained nodes. Since we are removing node's subtree, we need to ensure that after removal, for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol not in the subtree of node. Equivalent: for each required symbol, there exists at least one node in the tree outside node's subtree that has that symbol OR inside the subtree but not removed? Actually if a required symbol node is inside the subtree, it would be removed, so we need another node with same symbol outside the subtree. So we need to precompute global symbol counts, and also counts per subtree? Alternative: For each required symbol, we can check if there exists any node with that symbol that is not inside node's subtree. Since we process many nodes, we could maintain a set of nodes that are still present for each symbol. But to efficiently check, we can compute the subtree nodes for each node (by traversing children). Since number of nodes is small (in fixture, 7 nodes), we can just recompute by scanning retained set each time. However, repeated scans may be okay for small cases. But for generality, we can precompute subtree node sets.\n\n   - We'll perform the check by temporarily creating a retained set that excludes subtree of node. Then check each required symbol: exists node with that symbol in retained set. If yes, then removal is feasible.\n\n   - If feasible, then we remove node: add node.id to removal_order list, and add node and all its descendants to removed_set. Also update retained set? We'll just maintain removed_set.\n\n   - Note: when we remove node, its descendants become removed as well, but those descendants might have been processed already (since deeper). If a descendant was originally removable and we processed it earlier but decided not to remove because of required symbols? Actually we processed it earlier; maybe we didn't remove it because removal would have removed required symbols. But if later we remove an ancestor, that descendant will be removed too, which could remove required symbols. That's why the tentative check must consider that removal of ancestor includes its whole subtree. So the check must be done at the time of removal, not relying on previous decisions because those might have been local. So the algorithm: we consider nodes in descending depth order. For each node, we decide whether to remove it, but we must ensure that the removal does not cause loss of required symbols considering all nodes currently retained (including those that are not removed yet but are deeper and might be retained). Since we process deeper first, when we get to an ancestor, some of its descendants might have been removed already (if we removed them earlier), so their removal is already accounted in removed_set. So the tentative check should consider the current removed_set plus the new subtree removal. That's correct.\n\n   - Implementation steps:\n\n        - Create dict node_by_id.\n        - Build children list for each parent.\n        - Compute all_nodes set.\n        - removed_set = set()\n        - removal_order = []\n        - Precompute subtree nodes for each node? Or compute on fly using DFS from node limited to nodes not removed? But since we may remove nodes, subtree nodes might include already removed nodes? Actually if a descendant is already removed, it's in removed_set, so we should not consider it as part of node's retained children. But when we tentatively remove node, its subtree includes all descendants, even those already removed? In a parse tree, removal of node removes its entire subtree, regardless of whether some descendants were already removed? Typically if you remove a node, you remove the whole subtree, so any previously removed descendants are already gone. So for the purpose of checking required symbols, we only care about nodes that are still present. So we can consider the set of nodes that are currently retained: all_nodes - removed_set. Then the subtree of node (including node) consists of all nodes that are descendants of node (including node) that are still in retained. But if some descendants were already removed, they are not in retained, so they don't matter. So when we tentatively remove node, we will remove from retained the set: {node} union {descendants of node that are currently retained}. That's equivalent to the set of nodes in the subtree that are still present. We can compute by traversing from node using children links but only include nodes not in removed_set.\n\n        - So algorithm for tentative check:\n            - If node has any child that is not removed (i.e., child.id not in removed_set), then we cannot remove because it has retained children. (Already checked)\n            - But what about grandchildren? They are also children? Actually if all children are removed, then all deeper descendants are also removed because we process bottom-up. However, it's possible that a child is not removable (e.g., required) and thus not removed, so we cannot remove node. So condition \"all children removed\" ensures that all descendants are removed. So we can check only direct children for retained status; due to bottom-up, if a child is removed, its descendants are also removed. So condition is sufficient.\n\n            - Then compute retained symbols: count of nodes per required symbol in the set of retained nodes (all_nodes - removed_set). Then compute after removal: new_retained = retained - subtree_currently_present (node and its descendants still retained). But since all children removed, subtree_currently_present is just {node}. Because all descendants are already removed. So we only need to check if node's own symbol is required and there is another node with same symbol. Actually if node's children are all removed, then its entire subtree is already gone. So removing node only removes itself. So tentative check reduces to: if node.symbol in required_symbols, then we cannot remove because it would lose that symbol (unless another node with same symbol exists in retained). But we already said we can't remove nodes with required symbol (that condition is separate). So in that case, we skip due to node.symbol in required_symbols. So if node's symbol is not required, then removing node doesn't affect required symbols because all children removed and node is not required. So the tentative check is automatically satisfied if node has no retained children and node.symbol not required. But wait: what if node.symbol is not required, but some descendant had a required symbol that was already removed earlier? That's fine because required symbol is gone anyway. But we must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still represented somewhere in the tree (i.e., in nodes not removed). If a required symbol existed only in a descendant that we already removed, then that symbol is already missing. But our algorithm would have prevented removal of that descendant if it would cause loss. So that required symbol should have been preserved earlier. So when processing node, all required symbols are present in retained set. Removing node (non-required) does not remove any required symbol because no required symbol in subtree (all descendants removed and none had required? Actually descendants might have had required but if we removed them earlier, they are gone and we would have ensured there is another copy because we checked at that time. So it's safe.\n\n            - However, consider scenario where a descendant had a required symbol but we chose not to remove it because there was no other copy. That descendant is retained. Then its ancestor cannot have all children removed because that descendant is still there. So ancestor cannot be removed. So the condition \"all children removed\" will fail. So the algorithm should work.\n\n        - Therefore, we can simplify: For a node to be removable, it must have all its children already removed (i.e., in removed_set), and its symbol not in required_symbols, and it is not root, and removable=True. Then we can remove it without further check on required symbols? But we must still check that after removal, all required symbols remain. Since the only removal is the node itself (non-required), and all required symbols are elsewhere (maybe in other branches), it's fine. However, what if a required symbol is present only in the node itself? That would be blocked by node.symbol not in required_symbols. So no issue.\n\n        - But what if the node's subtree contains some nodes that are not removed yet (contrary to \"all children removed\")? As argued, that would prevent removal. So condition is sufficient.\n\n        - But there is a nuance: what if a node has children that are removed but those children had retained grandchildren? That cannot happen because if a child is removed, its entire subtree is removed (we add all descendants to removed_set). So no.\n\n        - So the algorithm: Process nodes in descending depth, for each node:\n            - if node.id in removed_set: continue\n            - if not node.removable: continue\n            - if node.parent is None: continue\n            - if node.symbol in required_symbols: continue\n            - check if all direct children of node are in removed_set (or no children). If yes:\n                - then remove node: add node.id to removal_order, add node and all its descendants (that are still not removed? but all descendants should already be in removed_set because children removed bottom-up, but to be safe, we can add node and all its descendants recursively, but they are likely already in removed_set. However, to handle case where some descendant was not removed (if it had a required symbol and we didn't remove it) then that descendant is not removed, so its parent won't have all children removed. So we don't need to add descendants unnecessarily.\n\n                - So just add node.id to removed_set and removal_order.\n\n        - After processing all nodes, retained_ids = sorted([node.id for node in nodes if node.id not in removed_set])\n        - Return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nBut wait: the fixture includes \"required_symbols\" as list [\"root\", \"s1\"]. So nodes with symbol \"root\" (root itself) and \"s1\" cannot be removed. The root has removable false anyway.\n\nTest on first case: nodes: root (depth0), n1(s1,d1,removable false), n2(s2,d1,removable true), n3(s0,d1,removable false), n4(s1,d2,removable true, parent n2), n5(s2,d2,removable false, parent n3), n6(s0,d2,removable true, parent n1). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nProcess by depth: deepest depth2: n4, n5, n6. n4: removable true, symbol s1 is required? s1 is in required_symbols, so cannot remove (skip). n5: removable false, skip. n6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, parent n1. Check children: none (depth2 leaf). All children removed? yes vacuously. So remove n6. removed_set={n6}, removal_order=[n6].\nThen depth1 nodes: n1, n2, n3. n1: removable false, skip. n2: removable true, symbol s2 not required, parent root. Children: n4? n4 is child of n2. Is n4 in removed_set? No, n4 is not removed (because we skipped it). So condition \"all children removed\" fails. So cannot remove n2. n3: removable false, skip.\nThen depth0 root: not removable, skip.\nSo final removed_set: {n6}, retained_ids: all others sorted: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']. But root might be sorted as 'root'? IDs are strings. Sorted alphabetically: ['n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','root']? Actually 'root' vs 'n...': 'r' > 'n', so 'root' comes after. But typical sort as strings: compare 'n1' and 'root': 'n' < 'r', so 'n1' before 'root'. So list: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root. That seems fine. removal_order = ['n6'].\n\nNow is that correct? Should we have removed n6? It is removable, not required, leaf. Removing it does not affect required symbols. So yes. But check if n6 is removable? Yes. The algorithm works.\n\nSecond case: similar, will remove n6 if removable? In case 1, n6 utility 0.195..., but we don't use utility. So will remove n6. In case 2, n6 is removable true, symbol s0, not required, leaf. So removed. In case 3, same.\n\nBut is that all? The problem mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"interestingness oracle\". However, we are not given grammar or oracle. The description says \"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we are to implement the algorithm without actual grammar/oracle, only using the provided fields: nodes, required_symbols. The \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\" are likely markers that we must ensure our decisions are valid based on the provided data. But we don't have grammar rules. Perhaps the \"utility\" field? No. I think the task is to implement the reduction algorithm as described: consider deeper nodes first, check removal conditions (cannot remove root, required symbols, nodes with retained children, and must keep all required symbols). That's it. The \"interestingness oracle\" is implicitly satisfied by preserving required symbols and not removing nodes that have retained children? Not sure.\n\nBut there is also mention of \"replacement candidates\" but not used. The algorithm seems to be only removal. So I'll implement as above.\n\nNow ensure we validate required fields: check that each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols is list of strings. Raise ValueError if missing or invalid types.\n\nEdge cases: If there is no node with a required symbol? The input is presumed valid.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable object: dictionary with keys \"retained_ids\" (list of strings) and \"removal_order\" (list of strings). Sorted retained_ids alphabetically.\n\nNow produce Python source code. Must not mutate case. We'll copy payload.\n\nWrite `solve(case)` that extracts case['payload'].\n\nNow format as JSON string with escaped newlines, etc.\n\nImplementation code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get('payload')\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing or invalid payload\")\n    nodes = payload.get('nodes')\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing or invalid nodes\")\n    required_symbols = payload.get('required_symbols')\n    if not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing or invalid required_symbols\")\n    # Validate each node\n    for node in nodes:\n        if not isinstance(node, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node not a dict\")\n        for key in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility'):\n            if key not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Node missing field {key}\")\n        if not isinstance(node['id'], str):\n            raise ValueError(\"id not string\")\n        if node['parent'] is not None and not isinstance(node['parent'], str):\n            raise ValueError(\"parent not string or None\")\n        if not isinstance(node['symbol'], str):\n            raise ValueError(\"symbol not string\")\n        if not isinstance(node['depth'], int):\n            raise ValueError(\"depth not int\")\n        if not isinstance(node['removable'], bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"removable not bool\")\n        if not isinstance(node['utility'], (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"utility not number\")\n    # Build map\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    # Build children list\n    children = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        p = node['parent']\n        if p is None:\n            if root_id is not None:\n                raise ValueError(\"Multiple roots\")\n            root_id = node['id']\n        else:\n            if p not in node_map:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {p} not found\")\n            children[p].append(node['id'])\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root\")\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed_set = set()\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid in removed_set:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if node['parent'] is None:  # root\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check all children are removed\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        if not all(cid in removed_set for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        # Check that all required symbols are still present after removal\n        # Since we remove only this node (all descendants already removed), we just check if after removing this node,\n        # every required symbol still has at least one node.\n        # We'll compute retained set after removal: all nodes except removed_set union {nid}\n        # But since nid not in removed_set yet.\n        retained = set(node_map.keys()) - removed_set - {nid}\n        # For each required symbol, check if any node with that symbol remains in retained\n        ok = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            if not any(node_map[rid]['symbol'] == sym for rid in retained):\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        # Remove node\n        removed_set.add(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set])\n    return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n```\n\nBut note: In the loop, we are checking all required symbols remain after removal. This is redundant given our earlier reasoning that if node has no retained children and symbol not required, removal of node alone cannot remove a required symbol because any required symbol must be elsewhere. However, there is a corner case: if a required symbol appears only in nodes that are descendants of node and those descendants are already removed? If they were already removed, they are not in retained, so removing node doesn't affect. If they are still retained (because we didn't remove them earlier due to required condition), then those descendants would be in child subtree and child not removed, so condition \"all children removed\" fails. So the check is indeed unnecessary but safer to keep. However, we must consider that a descendant that had a required symbol might have been removed because there was another copy elsewhere? But if it had a required symbol and was removable, we could have removed it if there was another copy. That is possible: e.g., two nodes with same required symbol, one is deeper, we remove it, then later we try to remove ancestor. In that case, the required symbol is still present in the other copy. So removal of ancestor (which is not required) is fine. So the check is still fine.\n\nBut the condition \"all children removed\" already ensures that any subtree with required symbols is either already removed (because we removed those nodes when they were processed) or not present (no such nodes). So the extra check is not needed. But we include for correctness in case of weird scenarios.\n\nNow, what about nodes that are not removable but have removable children? We don't remove them. Fine.\n\nThe output format: \"sorted retained IDs\" – we sort alphabetically. That seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to ensure the code uses only standard library, no file access, etc.\n\nNow write the JSON with escaped newlines and quotes.\n\nFinal output: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n ...\"}\n\nWe'll produce the JSON string. Let's write the code with proper indentation and escape.\n\nI'll write the code as a single line with \\n for newlines, escaped backslashes, quotes.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll generate the JSON manually.\n\nThe code as one string:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case.get('payload')\\n    if not isinstance(payload, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('Missing or invalid payload')\\n  